    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/University-MS/Instructor---Mechanical-Engineering_JR103137-1": {
      "title": "Instructor - Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Mechanical Engineering Job Summary: Performs the function of teaching with renewable contracts. Not eligible for tenure. Job Description: Position Description The Department of Mechanical Engineering at the University of Mississippi (Ole Miss), Oxford, Mississippi, invites applications for a full-time Instructor of Mechanical Engineering. This teaching-focused, non-tenure-track faculty appointment will strengthen hands-on undergraduate instruction in mechanical engineering laboratories and related engineering courses. The successful candidate will teach and support hands-on undergraduate laboratories, including structures and dynamics, materials, thermal-fluids, instrumentation, design, or related mechanical engineering laboratory experiences. The Instructor will work closely with faculty course coordinators, laboratory faculty directors, department staff, and the machine shop supervisor to support safe, effective, well-maintained, and practice-oriented instructional laboratories. Primary Responsibilities Teach, coordinate, or support hands-on undergraduate mechanical engineering laboratories, with possible assignments in Structures and Dynamics Lab, Materials Lab, Thermal-Fluids Lab, instrumentation, design, or related laboratory courses. Teach undergraduate mechanical engineering or engineering science courses as assigned, based on departmental needs and the candidate's expertise. Develop, revise, and assess laboratory manuals, experiments, safety procedures, demonstrations, project-based activities, and course materials. Work collaboratively with faculty laboratory directors, course coordinators, department staff, and the machine shop supervisor to support laboratory safety, equipment readiness, maintenance coordination, and continuous improvement. Mentor undergraduate students in laboratory practice, engineering problem solving, professional conduct, and safe use of equipment and facilities. Contribute to ABET-related assessment, curriculum coordination, laboratory documentation, department service, and other continuous-improvement activities. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Required Qualifications Master’s degree in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related engineering field by the appointment start date. Relevant laboratory, instructional, technical, or hands-on engineering experience. Demonstrated ability or strong potential to teach and supervise hands-on undergraduate engineering laboratory experiences. Strong written and oral communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and students. Commitment to safe, student-centered, inclusive, and professionally rigorous engineering education, as demonstrated through teaching, mentoring, laboratory, professional, or related experience. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related engineering field. Experience teaching, coordinating, or supporting undergraduate laboratories in structures and dynamics, materials, thermal-fluid sciences, instrumentation, design, manufacturing, or related mechanical engineering areas. Experience developing or modernizing laboratory experiments, safety documentation, assessment tools, lab manuals, or project-based learning activities. Experience working with machine shop, fabrication, instrumentation, testing, or mechanical systems in an instructional, industrial, or applied engineering setting. Evidence of student-centered teaching, undergraduate mentoring, course assessment, ABET-related documentation, or structured curriculum improvement. Application Procedures Application materials should include: A cover letter addressing qualifications, laboratory teaching interests, industry or professional engineering experience, and fit with the department's instructional needs. A current curriculum vitae. A statement describing laboratory teaching experience, instructional approach, and experience with hands-on, project-based, safety-focused, or practice-oriented engineering education. Names and contact information for three professional references. Questions regarding the position may be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Damian Stoddard, at dlstodda@olemiss.edu. Education Qualifications: Master's (Required) Experience: Related Experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/University-MS/Assistant-Associate-Professor-in-Physics-Education-Research_JR103118": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Physics Education Research",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-18\nPosted: Posted 17 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: The successful candidate will be a regular faculty member in Physics and Astronomy. Job Summary: The Department of Physics and Astronomy at the University of Mississippi seeks highly qualified candidates for a tenure-track faculty position in physics education research. The role involves teaching (undergraduate and graduate courses), research (including supervision of students, especially in projects leading to master’s and doctoral degrees), and service (to the department, university, community, profession). Job Description: The Department of Physics and Astronomy at the University of Mississippi (UM) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in physics education research (PER) at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. This position is supported in part by an endowment to honor F. Douglas and Cora Beal Shields and represents a significant institutional investment in establishing PER as a strategic area of excellence.   The purpose of the endowment is to recruit and retain outstanding faculty for the Department of Physics and Astronomy; to advance the understanding of physics pedagogy and contribute to the field of PER; to raise the quality of instruction across the department; and to seed a new research area with meaningful doctoral production. This position offers a unique opportunity to establish a nationally visible PER group at a flagship R1 university with strong undergraduate and graduate physics programs and to help define the future direction of PER at the University of Mississippi.   The position includes a competitive salary and a startup package. Income from the endowment will be used to provide funds for research and creative activity, including but not limited to support for personnel (graduate teaching and research assistants, undergraduate learning assistants), operational support for scientific studies, travel to conferences, and hosting visitors and workshops. There are also opportunities for collaboration and support in connection with UM’s Center for Excellence in Teaching and Learning, Center for Math and Science Education, Center for STEM Learning, and Institute for Data Science.   The Physics and Astronomy faculty are eager to attract a driven, energetic colleague who can help to craft a long-term vision for growing out PER in the department. Ideally, the new faculty member will be a proselytizer for active learning and a catalyst for extending modern, evidence-based practices. Like all our tenure-track faculty, this person must also excel at mentoring graduate students and providing them with research opportunities that lead to the Ph.D. degree.   The search committee’s aperture for evaluating the candidates is quite wide. Applications are encouraged from researchers working in all areas of PER and at all career stages. The department is open to recruiting an emerging scholar or an established researcher who could be hired with an accelerated tenure clock.   A Ph.D. in Physics or a closely related field is required. Candidates should have relevant experience beyond the doctoral degree, a strong record of publication or impact in the field, demonstrated potential for establishing and sustaining a nationally recognized research program, and well-conceived plans to pursue external funding opportunities. The successful candidate is expected to establish an active and independent research program, teach effectively at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, and contribute to the service missions of the department and university.   Applications should be submitted online via the Faculty Career Site portal, which is accessible from https://olemiss.edu/hr/um-careers/ by clicking through to External Applicants > Faculty Openings. A complete application comprises a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement (up to 2 pages), research statement (up to 5 pages), and three (3) confidential letters of recommendation. Evaluation of applicants will begin on Friday, November 6 and continue until the position is filled. Questions should be sent to persearch@phy.olemiss.edu . Applicants’ recommenders can be directed to submit their letters to that same email address.   –––   Founded in 1848, the University of Mississippi (UM), affectionately known to alumni, students and friends as Ole Miss, is Mississippi's flagship university. Included in the elite group of “R1: Very High Research Spending and Doctorate Production” by the Carnegie Classification, it has a long history of producing leaders in public service, academics, and business. The University of Mississippi, consistently named by The Chronicle of Higher Education as a “Great College to Work For,” is located in Oxford, MS, which is ranked one of the “Top 10 Best College Towns.” With more than 24,000 students, UM is the state's largest university and is ranked among the nation's fastest-growing institutions. The University of Mississippi, which has aggressively implemented many health and wellness initiatives for its more than 2,900 employees, has consistently been named one of Mississippi’s Healthiest Workplaces.   Touted as the “Cultural Mecca of the South,” creativity abounds in Oxford as musicians, artists, and writers alike find inspiration in Oxford’s rich history, small-town charm and creative community. Oxford is a one-hour drive south of Memphis, TN and is known as the home of Nobel Prize winning author William Faulkner. Over the years Oxford has also been known for offering exceptional culinary experiences and as the home of the University of Mississippi and the Ole Miss Rebels, there is always something here to immerse yourself in. Oxford has also been featured as a literary and arts destination in such publications as The New York Times, Southern Living, Condé Nast Traveler, and GQ. Among other cultural activities, annual events include the Oxford Film Festival, a thriving local music scene, and the Ford Center Performing Arts Series. Oxford is a vibrant university town, filled with unique shops and galleries, eclectic restaurants and clubs, historic landmarks, and comfortable inns.   The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, national origin, ethnicity, citizenship, age, disability, military status, protected veteran status, genetic information, or any other legally protected status. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Teach a standard load of undergraduate and graduate physics courses. Build and sustain an active, high-impact research program in Physics Education Research that publishes regularly in relevant scientific journals, involves undergraduate and graduate students, and leads to significant graduate degree production. Contribute to departmental and university committees, engage in public outreach activities, and/or provide service to the profession. Education Qualifications: PhD: Physics (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/University-MS/Tenure-Track-Faculty-Position-in-Organic-Organometallic-Synthesis_JR102983": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Faculty Position in Organic/Organometallic Synthesis",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-18\nPosted: Posted 17 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry at the University of Mississippi is a vibrant and growing unit with excellent research infrastructure, including modern instrumentation and core facilities. Ole Miss offers a highly collaborative research environment and strong institutional support for new faculty. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until an adequate applicant pool is established. For full consideration, applicants are encouraged to submit their materials by October 15, 2026. Job Summary: The successful candidate will develop and maintain a vigorous, externally funded research program in chemistry, teach undergraduate and graduate courses in the Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry, mentor undergraduate and graduate students, and contribute service to the department, college, university, and chemistry-discipline. Job Description: The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry at the University of Mississippi invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor starting in August 2027. We seek an outstanding organic/organometallic synthetic chemist who will establish a vigorous, externally funded research program, excel in teaching at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, and contribute to departmental, college, and university service. Individuals with expertise in any area of organic or organometallic chemistry, especially with research interests in synthetic chemistry of macromolecules and/or green/sustainable chemistry, and in those areas complementing active research programs in the Department, are strongly encouraged to apply. Applicants must have a Ph.D. and relevant postdoctoral experience in synthetic chemistry or a closely related field. The Department has 17 tenured and tenure-track faculty, 10 teaching faculty, and a vibrant graduate Ph.D. program. Construction of a new $175M STEM education facility was completed in 2024 fostering interdisciplinary opportunities to fulfill our commitment to excellence in chemical education. The successful candidate is expected to establish a nationally recognized, externally-funded research program; provide mentorship for graduate and undergraduate researchers; demonstrate excellence in teaching; and perform service to the department, college, university, and community ( https://visitoxfordms.com/ ). Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until an adequate applicant pool is established. Applications must be submitted online (careers.olemiss.edu) and should include a cover letter, a current CV, research plans, and a teaching philosophy statement. During the online application process, applicants will be prompted to provide the names and email addresses of three professional references that will provide letters of recommendation. The University of Mississippi has a Carnegie classification of R1 and is rated a “Great Colleges to Work For” by the Chronicle of Higher Education. The University of Mississippi is an EOE / AA / Minority / Female / Vet / Disability / Sexual Orientation / Gender Identity / Title VI / Title VII / Title IX / 504 / ADA / ADEA employer. The successful candidate will be expected to: Develop and maintain a strong, independent research program that attracts external funding and produces high-quality scholarly publications. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in organic chemistry, including both lecture and laboratory components. Actively mentor and supervise undergraduate and graduate students in research. Contribute to service activities that support the mission of the department, university, and profession. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Establish and lead an independent, externally funded research program that produces high-impact scholarly publications and advances the field of chemistry or biochemistry. Teach assigned undergraduate and graduate courses (lecture and laboratory), develop new courses as needed, and incorporate modern pedagogical practices. Recruit, mentor, and supervise undergraduate research students, M.S. students, and Ph.D. students, including serving on thesis and dissertation committees. Actively seek and secure external research funding from federal agencies (e.g., NSF, NIH, DOE), foundations, and other sources. Participate in departmental governance, including serving on committees related to curriculum, instrumentation, safety, graduate admissions, and faculty searches. Contribute to college and university service through participation in broader academic committees and initiatives. Engage in professional service, such as reviewing manuscripts, grant proposals, and serving on conference or professional society committees. Education Qualifications: PhD: Chemistry (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/University-MS/Assistant-Associate-Professor-of-Counselor-Education_JR103082": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Counselor Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Department of Leadership and Counselor Education Job Summary: The Department of Leadership and Counselor Education at the University of Mississippi invites applications for a full-time, 9-month tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in the Counselor Education program. Job Description: The Counselor Education program is accredited by the Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (CACREP) and offers master's degrees in School Counseling and Clinical Mental Health Counseling, as well as a Ph.D. in Counselor Education and Supervision. Degree programs are offered in-person on the Oxford campus. The department also offers fully online Education Specialist (Ed.S.) programs in Play Therapy and School Counseling Leadership. The successful candidate will join a collaborative faculty committed to excellence in teaching, scholarship, clinical training, and service. Faculty in the program enjoy a collegial environment with opportunities to mentor master's and doctoral students, collaborate on interdisciplinary research, engage in grant-funded initiatives, and contribute to the continued growth of a nationally respected counselor education program. Faculty members typically teach a 2-2 course load during the academic year, with opportunities for summer teaching available. The University of Mississippi is home to a vibrant community of scholars dedicated to advancing knowledge through innovative research, exceptional teaching, and meaningful service. Located in the nationally acclaimed college town of Oxford, Mississippi, the university offers an intellectually engaging environment, strong institutional support for faculty success, and an outstanding quality of life. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Responsibilities include the following: teaching a 2:2 course load; research within the discipline; service to the department, college, university, and profession; student advising. The Department of Leadership & Counselor Education encourages a culture of presence. Required Qualifications An earned doctorate in Counselor Education and Supervision. ABD candidates will be considered; however, the doctoral degree must be conferred prior to the position start date. Candidates who are ABD may be asked to provide confirmation from their dissertation chair that the dissertation defense has been scheduled. Experience working with graduate counseling students through instruction, clinical supervision, and/or scholarly activities. Eligibility for licensure as a Licensed Professional Counselor (LPC) in the State of Mississippi. Preferred Qualifications Active involvement in professional counseling organizations (e.g., ACA, ACES, AMHCA, ASCA, state counseling associations, or related professional organizations). Evidence of a developing or established record of scholarly productivity, including publications, conference presentations, grant activity, or other scholarly contributions. Demonstrated commitment to mentoring graduate students; fostering supportive and collaborative learning environments; and engaging in meaningful service to the university, the counseling profession, and the communities we serve. Application Instructions: To be considered, applicants must apply online through the University of Mississippi employment system at https://careers.olemiss.edu . Applications should include: A cover letter describing the applicant's interest in the position and qualifications; Curriculum vitae; Graduate transcripts; Names and contact information for three professional references. Additional materials may be requested for the interview process. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled or an adequate applicant pool has been established. Questions regarding the position may be directed to Dr. Melanie Burgess, Search Committee Chair ( maburges@olemiss.edu ). Education Qualifications: PhD Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/University-MS/Assistant-Professor_JR102946": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 28 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: The Department of Psychology Job Summary: The Department of Psychology at the University of Mississippi seeks applicants for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Clinical Psychology. We are seeking faculty with child-focused research and clinical interests to further grow our graduate program. We are particularly interested in individuals who would broaden existing departmental strengths by adding expertise in areas such as neurodevelopmental disorders, feeding/eating disorders, developmental psychopathology, and childhood emotional disorders. We also welcome child-focused applicants outside of these areas. Applicants should have a strong record of research productivity and a plan for developing and maintaining a substantive research program that would attract external funding. Applicants must be willing to teach both graduate and undergraduate courses, pursue licensure to provide clinical supervision for graduate students, and recruit and mentor graduate students. About Our Programs The Psychology Department consists of 30 full-time faculty members and over 60 graduate students. Graduate programs include the APA-accredited doctoral program in Clinical Psychology and a doctoral program in Experimental Psychology. The Clinical Psychology program adheres to a scientist-practitioner model of training, with a particular focus on evidence-based approaches. Our program is a leading producer of PhDs in the College of Liberal Arts and has been continually accredited by the American Psychological Association since 1973 (reaccredited in 2021 for the maximum of 10 years). The Experimental Psychology program focuses on behavioral analysis (with new undergraduate emphasis area), cognitive, developmental, and social psychology. Faculty both within and across graduate programs frequently collaborate on research. Our graduate psychology programs were joint recipients of the 2021 Donald R. Cole Excellence in Promoting Inclusiveness in Graduate Education. https://psychology.olemiss.edu/ https://gradschool.olemiss.edu/funding-and-opportunities/awards-and-grants/ In addition to existing department facilities on the beautiful main campus, this summer the Department of Psychology expanded into 22,000 square feet of newly renovated space in the South Oxford Center (SOC). Department space at the SOC includes faculty offices and labs, administrative space, graduate workspaces, seminar rooms, and the Psychological Services Center (PSC), which has 9 therapy/assessment rooms. The SOC is a short drive from the main campus with regular bus service between locations, providing easy access and parking for community research participants. Job Description: Minimum Requirements Applicants must be committed to evidence-based practice and have completed – or be on track to complete – a PhD in Clinical Psychology and predoctoral internship from an APA-accredited program by the time of appointment in August 2027. Application Procedures All applications must be submitted online at http://careers.olemiss.edu/ and should include: a) a cover letter that addresses how your program of research and approach to and experience in teaching fit with the position; b) curriculum vitae; c) names and contact information for three references; d) up to three selected journal reprints or preprints; and e) two short statements (not to exceed two pages each), with one describing your program of research and one describing your philosophy on teaching and approach to clinical supervision. For ease of submissions, please upload all required documents into one PDF, to attach to your application. Questions about this position may be directed to the search committee chair Dr. Todd Smitherman, tasmithe@olemiss.edu. Review of materials will begin September 15, 2026 and continue until an adequate applicant pool is reached   Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Education Qualifications: PhD (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/University-MS/Assistant--Associate--or-Full-Clinical-Professor-of-Law--tenure-track-or-non-tenure-track---Director-of-the-Immigration-Clinic_JR103076": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Clinical Professor of Law (tenure-track or non-tenure-track), Director of the Immigration Clinic",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 28 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: School of Law Job Summary: Assistant, Associate, or Full Clinical Professor of Law (tenure-track or non-tenure-track), Director of the Immigration Clinic The University of Mississippi School of Law invites applicants with a passion for immigration law, including both teaching and representing live clients with immigration-associated legal issues, to establish and direct an Immigration Clinic. The Clinic’s mission will be to train students to protect the rights of both our local and state-wide immigrant community, and to engage in public service lawyering in the immigration policy field. Job Description: The Director’s status may either be tenure-track or long-term contract. The Director and the Law School Dean will reach a decision based upon the Director’s desire, the Clinc’s specific needs, as well as the Law School’s administrative and pedagogical needs. The position will be a twelve-month appointment. The Director’s primary responsibilities will include managing caseload, selecting cases, litigating cases, supervising students, and administering all aspects of the Clinic, including managing the Clinic budget and supervising Clinic staff. The successful candidate for Director will achieve excellence in some or all of the following, depending on the specific scope of contractual employment: scholarship, teaching, public advocacy, and service to the Law School and community consistent with his or her clinical responsibilities. We seek applicants with demonstrated accomplishments in immigration-related litigation, including some or all of the following: representing immigrants in removal proceedings, immigration-related complex federal litigation, representing asylees in asylum cases, and contributions to immigration policy and community education projects within their local communities. The Director should be prepared to partnerwith local, regional, and national civil rights and immigration-focused organizations, many of which are currently working in local and state immigrant communities in Mississippi. Applicants should have a minimum of five years of legal experience, which should include litigating within the areas of law referenced above. The Director should also possess staff management experience, junior attorney mentorship experience, or their equivalents. The Director must hold a J.D. degree from an American Bar Association-accredited law school or international equivalent. The Director must be admitted to the Mississippi Bar, obtain admission through reciprocity with his or her current state of licensure, or obtain admission through examination at the earliest practicable administration of the Mississippi Bar Examination. The successful candidate will demonstrate promise as a teacher, a commitment to clinical teaching and public interest law practice, and service. Any candidate pursuing a tenure-track appointment should have a record of scholarship or evidence of promise to produce scholarship that is consistent with the demands of clinical teaching and litigation. Applicants will be accepted until the position is filled or an adequate applicant pool is established. Interested persons must apply online at careers.olemiss.edu by submitting a cover letter, resume or curriculum vitae, and a list of at least three references. Direct applications are welcome and should be sent to Professor Martin Edwards, Chair of the Faculty Appointments Committee, at amedwar1@olemiss.edu. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Duties as assigned Education Qualifications: JD (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/University-MS/Assistant--Associate--or-Full-Professor-1_JR103051": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 28 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: School of Law Job Summary: The University of Mississippi School of Law invites applications and nominations for as many as two tenure-track professors to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. Appointments will be at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor depending on experience. We invite and encourage scholars in all fields to apply as our teaching needs are quite broad. We are most interested in candidates who wish to teach Torts or Civil Procedure. We are also interested in candidates who wish to teach other 1L and Bar-tested subjects such as Criminal Law and Family Law, and we may also consider candidates who wish to teach Criminal Procedure, Evidence, and Business and Commercial Law. Finally, we are always looking for candidates who can expand our course offerings in growing practice areas such as Health Law, Labor & Employment Law, and Insurance Law. Position Details Appointment: 9-Month Assignment Type: Tenure-Track Job Description: Tenure Track Faculty members perform the functions of teaching, research or creative activities, and service and are assigned to an academic unit appropriate for their Discipline descriptor. Tenure Track Faculty members are usually hired as assistant professors and enter the tenure track. For example, an assistant professor must serve a probationary period of five years and be reviewed for tenure during his or her sixth year of continuous employment at the University. Tenure Track Faculty members may be hired, particularly at the associate and full ranks, with a reduction in the tenure probationary period, as explained in the Tenure Policy. Tenure Track Faculty members at the Associate and Full ranks are full members of the Graduate Faculty, while those at the Assistant rank are Associate Members of the Graduate Faculty. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Qualifications: A candidate must have a J.D. degree from an ABA accredited law school, or equivalent international degree, and a commitment to teaching in an environment dedicated to excellence in teaching and mentoring of students. All applicants should have a distinguished academic background, and either demonstrated promise or a record of excellence in both scholarship and teaching. The most competitive candidates will have a record of published or accepted law review articles or equivalents and some record demonstrating potential for excellence in teaching. Application Instructions: To apply, please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references with your online application. We also welcome, but do not require, additional materials such as a research agenda or teaching statement. The position will be open until filled or an adequate applicant pool is reached. Questions are welcome, and should be directed to Professor Martin Edwards, Chair of the Faculty Appointments Committee, at amedwar1@olemiss.edu. Education Qualifications: JD (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/University-MS/Frank-R-Day-Mississippi-Bankers-Association-Endowed-Chair_JR102985": {
      "title": "Frank R. Day/Mississippi Bankers Association Endowed Chair",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-28\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: School of Business Administration- Department of Finance Job Summary: Performs the functions of teaching, research or creative activities, and service in the Department of Finance. Job Description: The Department of Finance in the School of Business Administration at The University of Mississippi invites applications for the Frank R. Day/Mississippi Bankers Association Endowed Chair. This position is at the rank of Associate or Full Professor. The appointment can be either tenured or non-tenure track and will start in August 2027. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate banking and finance courses and carry out duties assigned by the Dean of the School of Business Administration and the Chair of the Department of Finance. The standard teaching load for the tenured position is 2/1; the non-tenure track load is 3/3. Summer teaching opportunities are available for additional compensation, subject to course and funding availability. Tenure requires a strong record of both research and teaching. This chair carries significant service responsibilities that distinguish it from a traditional faculty appointment. The holder will serve as Director of the Mississippi School of Banking, which is a professional development program for banking practitioners, and will maintain an active relationship with the Mississippi Bankers Association. Additional responsibilities include managing the Banking Symposium, a Banking Symposium that regularly draws 200 or more attendees, along with other initiatives that elevate the banking industry's profile in Mississippi, the region, and nationally. To be considered, applicants must have earned a doctorate degree in finance from an AACSB accredited or well-regarded business program. Candidates must have demonstrated research and teaching interests in banking (finance). Candidates for a tenured position should possess a proven track record of original research and/or publication in top banking and finance journals. Candidates for a non-tenure track position must be academically qualified to teach banking and finance courses under AACSB accreditation standards. All candidates must provide evidence of a strong commitment to effective teaching and service. No candidate will be considered who does not meet the departmental AACSB standards for Scholarly Academics (SA). Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: The successful candidate will teach undergraduate banking and finance courses and carry out duties assigned by the Dean of the School of Business Administration and the Chair of the Department of Finance. The standard teaching load for the tenured position is 2/1; the non-tenure track load is 3/3. Summer teaching opportunities are available for additional compensation, subject to course and funding availability. Tenure requires a strong record of both research and teaching.   This chair carries significant service responsibilities that distinguish it from a traditional faculty appointment. The holder will serve as Director of the Mississippi School of Banking, which is a professional development program for banking practitioners, and will maintain an active relationship with the Mississippi Bankers Association. Additional responsibilities include managing the Banking Symposium, a Banking Symposium that regularly draws 200 or more attendees, along with other initiatives that elevate the banking industry's profile in Mississippi, the region, and nationally. Qualifications To be considered, applicants must have earned a doctorate degree in finance from an AACSB accredited or well-regarded business program. Candidates must have demonstrated research and teaching interests in banking (finance). Candidates for a tenured position should possess a proven track record of original research and/or publication in top banking and finance journals. Candidates for a non-tenure track position must be academically qualified to teach banking and finance courses under AACSB accreditation standards. All candidates must provide evidence of a strong commitment to effective teaching and service. No candidate will be considered who does not meet the departmental AACSB standards for Scholarly Academics (SA). Screening of applications will begin immediately. The position will be open until filled or until an adequate applicant pool is achieved. Apply online (only) at https://careers.olemiss.edu . Applications cannot be accepted in any other format. Candidates should submit a curriculum vitae, cover letter, and evidence of effective teaching. At least three references will be required from candidates invited to campus. For additional information, contact the search committee chair, Dr. Robert Van Ness, 329 Holman Hall, School of Business Administration, PO Box 1848, The University of Mississippi, University, MS 38677-1848 (rvanness@bus.olemiss.edu). Education Qualifications: PhD: Banking & Finance (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/University-MS/Instructional-Asst-Professor---Mechanical-Engineering_JR102964": {
      "title": "Instructional Asst Professor - Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-23\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Mechanical Engineering Job Summary: The Department of Mechanical Engineering at the University of Mississippi (Ole Miss), Oxford, Mississippi, invites applications for a full-time Instructional Assistant Professor of Mechanical Engineering. This teaching-focused, non-tenure-track faculty appointment will strengthen high-quality undergraduate instruction in mechanical engineering courses. Candidates from all areas of mechanical engineering will be considered. Candidates with expertise and teaching capacity in core undergraduate areas such as materials, mechanics, dynamics, thermal-fluid sciences, design, engineering systems, hands-on laboratories, or related mechanical engineering topics are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will join a collegial department committed to rigorous engineering education, student success, laboratory safety, continuous improvement, and preparing graduates for professional practice and advanced study. Job Description: Teach undergraduate mechanical engineering and engineering science courses, with assignments based on departmental needs and the candidate's expertise. Teach, coordinate, or support hands-on undergraduate laboratories, potentially including structures and dynamics, materials, thermal-fluids, instrumentation, design, or related laboratory experiences. Develop, revise, and assess course and laboratory materials that support active learning, project-based learning, engineering practice, and strong student outcomes. Work collaboratively with faculty course coordinators, laboratory faculty directors, department staff, and the machine shop supervisor to support safe, effective, and well-maintained instructional laboratories. Mentor undergraduate students, maintain accessible office hours, and contribute to student success, retention, and professional development activities. Participate in departmental service, curriculum coordination, ABET-related assessment and documentation, and other continuous-improvement activities. Engage in professional, scholarly, educational, or industry-facing activities that enrich teaching and advance the department's instructional mission, as appropriate for the appointment. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related engineering discipline by the appointment start date. Demonstrated potential for, or evidence of, effective undergraduate teaching in mechanical engineering or related engineering science areas. Ability to teach core undergraduate mechanical engineering or engineering science courses and to support hands-on or project-based learning. Strong written and oral communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and students. Commitment to safe, student-centered, inclusive, and professionally rigorous engineering education, as demonstrated through teaching, mentoring, laboratory, professional, or related experience. Preferred Qualifications Two or more years of teaching, laboratory teaching, instructional support, industry, or closely related professional engineering experience. Experience teaching or supporting courses in materials science, mechanics of materials, dynamics, thermal-fluid sciences, design, engineering systems, instrumentation, or related mechanical engineering topics. Experience developing, modernizing, or coordinating hands-on laboratories, including attention to safety, equipment readiness, assessment, and coordination with shop or technical staff. Evidence of student-centered teaching practices, active learning, project-based learning, or effective instruction in high-enrollment undergraduate engineering courses. Experience with course assessment, ABET-related documentation, curriculum improvement, or other structured continuous-improvement processes. Professional, industry, or research experience that connects mechanical engineering fundamentals to contemporary engineering practice. Application Procedures Application materials should include: A cover letter addressing qualifications, teaching interests, and fit with the department's instructional needs. A current curriculum vitae. A teaching statement describing undergraduate teaching experience, teaching philosophy, and experience with hands-on, laboratory, project-based, or practice-oriented engineering education. Names and contact information for three professional references. Questions regarding the position may be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Samrat Choudhury, at schoudhu@olemiss.edu. Education Qualifications: PhD (Required) Experience: Related Experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
